{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba docs/02-product-spec.md | sed -n '228,305p'\"","aggregated_output":"   228\t| DX7 | Zero runtime dependencies (reconsidered only with a measured comparison). Size targets, provisional until M1 measures a working slice: 40 KiB gzipped for everything, 25 KiB for a page that uses only `line`, 2 KiB for the React adapter, CSS included. | size-limit in CI on the packed distribution | D4 |\n   229\t| DX8 | Importing in Node without a DOM does not throw, and `validate()` runs there. Works under React StrictMode, including setup, cleanup and setup again. Works under a Content Security Policy without `unsafe-inline` or `unsafe-eval`, served with real headers. Labels and formatter output are inserted as text, never as HTML. A target that matches no element throws an error that names the selector. | CI tests | D1 |\n   230\t| DX9 | Semantic versioning. A deprecated API keeps working for at least one minor release and six months. At most one major version a year. The previous release's documented examples still compile and run against each release candidate. | Release policy; API report diff; upgrade test | D6 |\n   231\t| DX10 | Every runnable example in the documentation is compiled and rendered in CI from the packed distribution. | Docs build | D1 |\n   232\t| DX11 | MIT license for everything, including zoom and accessibility. Third-party notices are verified at release. | Repository; release check | D7 |\n   233\t| DX12 | A coding agent gets the first chart right. Given fixed prompts and trap fixtures (UTC-midnight ISO dates, numeric strings, unsorted rows, missing days, a misspelled field), an agent with only the published docs produces a chart with no error diagnostics that passes the reader criteria. Recorded as agent rehearsal, not as user evidence. | Scripted rehearsal at each release | D1, 1.2 |\n   234\t\n   235\t## 5. Performance budgets\n   236\t\n   237\tThese numbers are provisional. They are design targets taken from Phase 1's order-of-magnitude measurements, and they become release gates only after the benchmark harness built in M0 has measured a working Axylo slice in M1 and the limits have been recalibrated and frozen (plan, M1 and M6). Each budget names its workload, metric and statistic so that two people measuring the same build get the same verdict.\n   238\t\n   239\t| ID | Workload | Metric | Statistic | Provisional limit | Phase 1 reference |\n   240\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n   241\t| P1 | `trend` (3×120), default options, reference desktop | Main-thread task time from the call until the chart is complete | Median of 20 runs | 10 ms | Not comparable across libraries below about 35 ms with the Phase 1 method |\n   242\t| P2 | One series, 100,000 and 1,000,000 points, typed columns | Same | Median of 20 | 60 ms and 250 ms | See 01, section 5.3: uPlot and tuned Chart.js are the fastest measured |\n   243\t| P3 | 600-point window, one appended point per display frame at 60 Hz, driven by an independent clock | Main-thread task time per update; and frames later than 25 ms | Median; count per 300 frames | 2 ms; 0 | See 01, section 5.3 |\n   244\t| P4 | Pointer moved across a 100,000-point chart | Time from input to the frame showing the matching readout | 95th percentile | One frame (16.7 ms at 60 Hz) | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n   245\t| P5 | 24 `trend` charts created on one page | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 200 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n   246\t| P6 | Download | Minified and gzipped bytes, CSS included | Exact | DX7 | 01, section 1.4 |\n   247\t| P8 | 40 compact charts created on one page | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 80 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n   248\t| P7 | Memory | Bytes owned by Axylo beyond the caller's columns for 1,000,000 points; heap trend over 1,000 create–destroy cycles | Measured after forced collection | At most 1× the size of the caller's two columns; no upward slope | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n   249\t\n   250\tThree rules hold whatever the numbers become:\n   251\t\n   252\t1. Budgets apply to default options. Finding D8 showed libraries that are fast only for authors who know which options to set.\n   253\t2. Budgets are absolute, on named hardware profiles. Comparison libraries, uPlot above all, are measured in the same runs and published beside Axylo, but a ratio to another library is not a gate: it would reward leaving out work that library does not do.\n   254\t3. The accessibility layer is on in every measured configuration.\n   255\t\n   256\t## 6. Decisions and the findings behind them\n   257\t\n   258\t\"Status\" records what the reviews changed.\n   259\t\n   260\t| Decision | What decided it | Status after review |\n   261\t|---|---|---|\n   262\t| Diagnostics are a first-class feature with codes | R5: none of the ten named the field, the row or the problem. | Kept. Severity and delivery rewritten (3.5): readers are told too, and production builds are not silent. |\n   263\t| Calendar dates, instants and wall-clock times are different types | R3: given instants, six libraries show a daily value on a different day in some zones, which is correct for instants and wrong for dates; given date strings, most of them get it right. The gap is that nothing says which one the author meant. | Kept, sharpened. The test now separates the types, and a hint fires when instants look like dates. Default zone for instants: the viewer's, with the zone always visible. |\n   264\t| Accessibility is in the core, on by default | R11–R13. | Kept. The table is built on demand, not eagerly. |\n   265\t| The normalized data, not the drawing, is the source for values, table, keyboard and summary | Reviews: a simplified drawing cannot return the values it dropped. | New. |\n   266\t| No animation on first render | Reading starts at first paint, and with 24 charts loading at once motion competes with it. R14: four of seven animating libraries ignore reduced motion. | Kept, with the rationale restated. Update transitions stay capped at 200 ms. |\n   267\t| Label fitting is a tested invariant with a visible fallback | R2. | Changed. The absolute \"everything always fits\" was infeasible in a 240 px tile; it is now \"nothing overlaps, and nothing disappears without the reader being told\". Rotation comes before truncation. |\n   268\t| One logical table, three input forms | D2. | Changed from \"one data shape\": typed columns and wide rows are first-class. |\n   269\t| Three methods with a commit contract | D3. | Changed from \"one update method\": `append` and `zoom` were always there, and their ordering is now specified. |\n   270\t| Canvas marks with DOM text | D9: a canvas library is the speed reference. D10: one DOM node per point is slow. | **Reopened.** Phase 1 does not show that SVG is slow, only that one node per point is; a single SVG path per series was fast. M1 builds both mark renderers on the same scene and measures them (section 8). Canvas is the working assumption. |\n   271\t| Simplified drawing that preserves extremes, on by default | D8. | Kept for lines and areas, with the guarantee restated per pixel column and per run of valid samples. \"Every spike survives\" was wrong as written. |\n   272\t| Palette held to measurable thresholds | R7. | Kept as a screen, marked provisional. Marks carry a non-color cue in every mode, which the first draft did not require. |\n   273\t| Colors and fonts from CSS | R10. | Kept. Repaint triggers specified; `palette` option replaced by `colors` keyed by series name. |\n   274\t| Numeric strings | R5. | Changed: a whole column of plain decimal strings is parsed with a hint; mixed columns are not. |\n   275\t| Chart types | Judgment, then the owner's decision. | All five in 1.0, plus the compact variant (3.7). |\n   276\t| MIT, including zoom and accessibility | D7. | Kept. |\n   277\t| React adapter in 1.0 | Recharts leads npm installs among chart-level libraries. | Kept, and built early to test the core API. Downloads show where charts are written, not that Axylo will be wanted there. |\n   278\t| Budgets apply to defaults | D8. | Kept. The ratio to uPlot is no longer a gate. |\n   279\t| Zero runtime dependencies | D4. | Kept provisionally; time-zone arithmetic uses `Intl`. |\n   280\t\n   281\t## 7. Changes this revision rejected\n   282\t\n   283\t| Proposal | Why not |\n   284\t|---|---|\n   285\t| Test with screen-reader users, outside developers or design partners before release | One-person project with no participants. Recorded as a stated limit (3.8). Agent rehearsal (DX12) and developer rehearsal (A3) are used and labelled as such. |\n   286\t| Collect chart sizes and device profiles from beta deployments | A chart library should not phone home. Workload sizes come from the spec and from issue reports. |\n   287\t| Switch to horizontal bars automatically when labels do not fit | Changing orientation changes what the chart says at a glance; Axylo suggests it and tells the reader what is hidden. |\n   288\t| Remove the first-chart line-count criterion | Kept as a floor, and joined by DX12, which tests getting it right. |\n   289\t\n   290\t## 8. Decisions still open\n   291\t\n   292\tSettled by the owner on 2026-11-18: scatter is in 1.0; the compact variant is in 1.0; the mark renderer stays open until M1 measures both.\n   293\t\n   294\t| Question | Recommendation | Settled by |\n   295\t|---|---|---|\n   296\t| Canvas or SVG paths for marks? | Decide from the M1 comparison: P1, P2, P3, P5, bytes, memory, theme toggle, forced colors, print. | M1 record. |\n   297\t| Which keyboard and screen-reader pattern for the plot? | Decide from the M1 spike: three candidate patterns on the `trend` chart, rehearsed on the five pairs. | M1 record, confirmed in M4. |\n   298\t| Is the palette that meets A5 one people want to look at? | Hand-tune from the search output with the thresholds in CI. | M5. |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
