{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba bench/README.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Benchmark harness (M0)\n     2\t\n     3\tThe measurement method of [docs/04-plan-performance.md](../docs/04-plan-performance.md), sections 2, 3 and 6, in a\n     4\tfirst version. It runs the same workload through Axylo and comparison libraries in system Chrome. It accepts a timing\n     5\tonly for a chart that has been checked to show the right data, and it compares two configurations in randomized,\n     6\tbalanced blocks.\n     7\t\n     8\t## Build and run\n     9\t\n    10\t```bash\n    11\tnode bench/build.mjs                       # bundles the page and the adapters into bench/dist (esbuild)\n    12\tnode --test bench/test/*.test.mjs          # statistics, admission (with deliberately broken adapters), outcomes\n    13\t\n    14\t# baseline A against candidate B, 6 blocks (3 AB + 3 BA in random order), a fresh browser context per run\n    15\tnode bench/run.mjs --baseline uplot --candidate chartjs-tuned --workload first --size 1x100000 --blocks 6\n    16\tnode bench/run.mjs --baseline noop  --candidate uplot --workload stream --blocks 6\n    17\tnode bench/run.mjs --baseline uplot --candidate chartjs-tuned --workload page --blocks 6\n    18\t\n    19\t# A-versus-A: the same adapter in both slots builds the noise band for that workload\n    20\tnode bench/run.mjs --aa uplot --workload first --size 1x100000 --blocks 8\n    21\t# use that band when classifying a comparison on the same workload\n    22\tnode bench/run.mjs --baseline uplot --candidate chartjs-tuned --workload first --size 1x100000 --blocks 6 --noise <aa run id>\n    23\t\n    24\tnode bench/analyze.mjs <run id> [--noise <aa run id>]    # re-print or re-classify a history record\n    25\t\n    26\t# adapter parameters: id:key=value,key=value (here: Axylo's SVG mark renderer, development build)\n    27\tnode bench/run.mjs --baseline axylo-canvas --candidate axylo:renderer=svg --workload page --blocks 8\n    28\t# several A-versus-A runs: the band is their union\n    29\tnode bench/run.mjs ... --noise <aa run id>,<aa run id>\n    30\t\n    31\t# other workloads\n    32\tnode bench/run.mjs --baseline axylo-canvas --candidate axylo-svg --workload theme --blocks 8\n    33\tnode bench/run.mjs --baseline axylo-canvas --candidate axylo-svg --workload resize --size 3x120 --charts 24 --blocks 8\n    34\tnode bench/run.mjs --baseline axylo-canvas --candidate axylo-svg --workload first --size 1x100000 --dpr 2 --blocks 6\n    35\t\n    36\t# one CPU profile of a create workload, attributed to Axylo's pipeline stages\n    37\tnode bench/profile.mjs --adapter axylo:renderer=svg,build=dev --size 1x100000\n    38\t```\n    39\t\n    40\t`bench/results/m1-session.sh` is the M1 renderer-comparison session; `bench/results/make-m1-report.mjs` turns its\n    41\trecords into `bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md`.\n    42\t\n    43\t`run.mjs` rebuilds `bench/dist` first unless `--no-build` is given. Options: `--size SxN` (first, page), `--gaps`\n    44\t(5% missing in runs), `--charts` (page, default 24; resize, default 1), `--toggles` (theme, default 20), `--window`/`--samples` (stream, default 600/300),\n    45\t`--blocks` (even, default 6), `--settle` ms (default 500), `--timeout` ms per run (default 120000), `--seed`\n    46\t(block order), `--dpr` (1 or 2), `--headed`, `--history-dir`. Scripts in `bench/package.json` wrap the same commands.\n    47\t\n    48\tThe runner serves `bench/dist` from an HTTP server on 127.0.0.1 that lives only for the runner process. The build\n    49\tcopies `packages/axylo/dist/axylo.iife.min.js` and `axylo.iife.js` into `bench/dist/axylo/` and records their hashes:\n    50\tother work rebuilds the library while sessions run, so a session builds once and runs everything with `--no-build`,\n    51\tand every run measures the same bytes. It uses `playwright-core` and `esbuild` from the repository root and system Chrome\n    52\t(`channel: 'chrome'`). Nothing is downloaded.\n    53\t\n    54\t## Pieces\n    55\t\n    56\t| File | Role |\n    57\t|---|---|\n    58\t| `src/page.html`, `src/page.js` | The one benchmark page. `?adapter=…&workload=…&params={…}&settle=…` |\n    59\t| `src/fixtures.js` | Seeded, deterministic data (pure; the runner builds the same data in Node and compares a checksum) |\n    60\t| `src/admission.js` | The admission check (pure) |\n    61\t| `src/stats.js` | Per-run summaries, balanced order, paired bootstrap, the three outcomes (pure) |\n    62\t| `src/adapters/*.js` | `noop`, `uplot`, `chartjs`, `chartjs-tuned`, `axylo` |\n    63\t| `test/adapters/*.js` | Deliberately broken adapters, used by the tests only |\n    64\t| `lib/driver.mjs` | Server, browser, one run in a fresh context, machine record |\n    65\t| `run.mjs`, `analyze.mjs` | Runner and re-analysis |\n    66\t| `history/<run id>.json` | One file per runner invocation (see below) |\n    67\t\n    68\t### Adapter interface\n    69\t\n    70\tAn adapter module's default export has `id`, `library`, `version`, `config`, `available()` (returns\n    71\t`{ ok }` or `{ ok: false, reason }`), an optional `setup()` (run before the clock starts, for example to add CSS) and\n    72\t`create(container, { x, ys, names }, { window })`. `x` is a `Float64Array` of epoch milliseconds, `ys` one\n    73\t`Float64Array` per series with `NaN` for a missing value. `create` returns a handle with:\n    74\t\n    75\t- `ready`: a promise that resolves when the library itself reports that it has drawn the data (uPlot: its `draw`\n    76\t  hook; Chart.js: `afterRender` once no animation is running). The harness never guesses readiness from frames.\n    77\t- `append(x, ys)`: add one point per series and drop the oldest (streaming window).\n    78\t- `drawnNewestX()`: the newest x in the chart's state at its last draw, recorded from the library's own draw hook.\n    79\t- `describe()`: what the chart shows, read from the library's own scale and data objects (each adapter says exactly\n    80\t  what it reads in a comment): visible x range, visible y range, and per series the number of points held, oldest and\n    81\t  newest x, and the number of missing values.\n    82\t- `destroy()`.\n    83\t\n    84\tConverting the typed columns into what the library accepts (plain arrays, `{x, y}` objects, `null` for gaps) happens\n    85\tinside `create`, so it is part of the timed work for every library: the input is the same for all of them.\n    86\t\n    87\t### Adapters in this milestone\n    88\t\n    89\t- `noop`: the measurement floor. Same calls, draws nothing. Its numbers are the harness, the page and the browser.\n    90\t- `uplot`: default configuration. Only the options uPlot needs to draw at all are set (size, stroke colours, `ms: 1`\n    91\t  for millisecond timestamps); see the comment in the file.\n    92\t- `chartjs`: starter default (labels + data arrays, a time x axis, everything else default, animation included).\n    93\t- `chartjs-tuned`: the documented performance options that keep axes, legend and tooltip. Every option that differs,\n    94\t  with its reason and documentation page, is listed at the top of the file, including the visible differences\n    95\t  (no entry animation, no point markers) and one fidelity risk (min-max decimation skips `NaN`, so above its\n    96\t  threshold a gap can be drawn across; the admission check reads the held data and does not catch this).\n    97\t- `axylo-canvas`, `axylo-svg`, and `axylo` (parameters `renderer=canvas|svg`, `build=prod|dev`): load the production\n    98\t  script-tag build `axylo.iife.min.js` (or, with `build=dev`, the development build, for profiles only) as a classic\n    99\t  script with `globalThis.__AXYLO_BENCH__ = true`, and read readiness and the admission readback from\n   100\t  `chart.__bench`. The renderer is selected through Axylo's internal `__renderer` option. Streaming uses\n   101\t  `chart.append(rows, { keep: 600 })`. Without the hook the adapter reports `not-admitted`.\n   102\t\n   103\tAdapter parameters: `--baseline id:key=value,...` passes `{ key: value }` to the adapter's `load`, `setup` and\n   104\t`create` (`opts.params`), and is recorded with every run. An adapter may also implement `load(params)` (async, before\n   105\tanything else, for example to load a script) and `configFor(params)` (the configuration label in the record).\n   106\t\n   107\t## Workloads\n   108\t\n   109\t| Workload | What runs | Containers |\n   110\t|---|---|---|\n   111\t| `first` | Create one line chart from typed columns. Sizes `3x120`, `1x10000`, `1x100000`, `1x1000000` (any `SxN` works). `--gaps`: 5% of each series missing, in runs | one 800×400 |\n   112\t| `page` | Create 24 charts of 3×120 at once (each its own seed) | 24 of 230×115 in a 6×4 grid |\n   113\t| `stream` | A 600-point window; then 300 samples from a producer on an independent 60 Hz clock | one 800×400 |\n   114\t| `theme` | One chart (default 3×120); then 20 toggles of a class-based dark theme on `<html>` (`html.bench-dark` sets `color-scheme: dark` and dark page colors) | one 800×400 |\n   115\t| `resize` | One chart of 1×100,000 (or `--charts 24 --size 3x120`); then the box holding it is animated 1,200 → 400 → 1,200 px over 2 s, one width per frame | 1,200×400, or a 6-column grid of 115 px rows |\n   116\t\n   117\tContainers are 2:1 because that is Chart.js's default aspect ratio, so its starter config fills them like the others.\n   118\tThe viewport is 1,440×900, device pixel ratio 1 unless `--dpr 2`. Points are a day apart up to 1,000 points and a\n   119\tminute apart above. Series are seeded random walks. One extreme value is injected into series 0 (at 37% of the x\n   120\trange; for streaming into a sample that stays in the final window), so a chart that clips or pins its y range fails\n   121\tadmission. Gaps never touch the first or last point or the extreme.\n   122\t\n   123\tThe streaming producer is a Web Worker with its own timers. Sample *k* is scheduled at `start + k × 16.67 ms`; the\n   124\tworker posts its sequence number, scheduled time and send time (converted to the page's clock). It does not depend on\n   125\tthe page's main thread or animation frames, so when the page falls behind, samples queue up and show as backlog.\n   126\t\n   127\t## Admission check\n   128\t\n   129\tBefore an adapter is timed on a workload, the runner does one untimed run and compares `describe()` with facts\n   130\tderived in Node from the fixture (`src/admission.js`). The same check runs again on every timed run, after its\n   131\tmeasurement window. It requires:\n   132\t\n   133\t- the visible x range contains the data's x extent, and is not wider than that extent plus 10% per side;\n   134\t- the visible y range contains the data's y extent (and so the injected extreme, which is also checked on its own),\n   135\t  and its span is at most 4× the larger of the data's y span and its largest |y| (a zero-based axis passes, a\n   136\t  runaway range does not);\n   137\t- the number of series, and per series the number of points held (600 for the streaming window), the oldest and\n   138\t  newest x to the millisecond, and the number of missing values;\n   139\t- the page built the same data as the runner (FNV-1a checksum of every column).\n   140\t\n   141\tAn adapter that fails the untimed admission run is recorded as `not-admitted` with the reasons and nothing is timed\n   142\tfor that comparison. A timed run that fails is recorded as `not-admitted`, its timing is discarded and it is not\n   143\tpaired. The floor adapter is recorded as admitted with \"not applicable\".\n   144\t\n   145\tWhat the check does not do yet: compare a screenshot with a reference (plan 3.1, sampled runs), check what was drawn\n   146\tas opposed to what the chart holds (for example a decimated drawing that bridges a gap), or check tooltip text.\n   147\t\n   148\t## Metrics\n   149\t\n   150\tAll come from one run in a fresh browser context. All are \"lower is better\".\n   151\t\n   152\t**Main-thread task time** (`taskMs`, primary for `first` and `page`): the change in Chrome's `TaskDuration`\n   153\t(CDP `Performance.getMetrics`) from just before the create call until a fixed settle window (500 ms) after the\n   154\tadapter reported ready. It counts main-thread work wherever the library schedules it (synchronous, microtask, timer,\n   155\tanimation frame, ResizeObserver), including Chart.js's one-second entry animation.\n   156\tIt does not include GPU-process or compositor work, raster on other threads, or anything after the settle window.\n   157\tIt includes a small constant from the harness itself (the two CDP calls, the idle settle window); the `noop` floor\n   158\tshows how much. It is time spent, not time waited: a chart can be cheap in task time and still appear late.\n   159\t\n   160\t`scriptMs` and `layoutStyleMs` are `ScriptDuration` and `LayoutDuration + RecalcStyleDuration` in the same window.\n   161\tThey are a breakdown, not a sum that must equal `taskMs`: Chrome attributes some work to other buckets (for example\n   162\tcode that runs inside a DevTools command task is counted under `DevToolsCommandDuration`, which is why the measured\n   163\twork starts inside a fresh task). `readyMs` is wall-clock time to the ready signal and `readyFrameMs` to the next\n   164\tanimation frame after it; neither is validated against a screenshot yet, and `readyFrameMs` mostly reflects where in\n   165\tthe frame the ready signal fell.\n   166\t\n   167\t**Streaming** (primary: `taskMsPerUpdate`): `TaskDuration` from just before the producer starts until the frame in\n   168\twhich the last sample became visible, divided by the number of samples. Also per run:\n   169\t\n   170\t- `delayP50Ms`, `delayP95Ms`, `delayMaxMs`: per sequence number, the time from the sample's scheduled production\n   171\t  time to the animation-frame callback in which the chart's own draw record includes it. The callback that reads\n   172\t  the record is registered after the append that needed it, so it runs after any frame callback the library itself\n   173\t  registered for that append. This is the frame in which the chart's state includes the sample, not the moment the\n   174\t  pixels reached the screen (presentation time needs a trace, not done yet). Because the producer and the display\n   175\t  are two independent 60 Hz clocks, the median delay of a run carries a phase offset between 0 and one frame that\n   176\t  differs from run to run; compare it across runs, not within one.\n   177\t- `maxBacklog`: the most samples whose scheduled time had passed at the start of a frame but which were not shown\n   178\t  after that frame.\n   179\t- `coalesced`: samples first shown in the same frame as a later sample (they were never on screen as the newest).\n   180\t  The producer runs at 60.00 Hz and this display at about 59.9 Hz, so when a run's phase sits near a frame boundary\n   181\t  samples alternate between frames and 30–40 coalesced samples with a backlog of 1 appear even for a cheap chart\n   182\t  (seen for uPlot and tuned Chart.js in the first session). Small backlog and coalescing counts are therefore mostly\n   183\t  clock beat; large backlogs, delays of several frames and frames over the threshold are the signal.\n   184\t- `frameP95Ms`, `frameP99Ms`, `framesOverThreshold`: intervals between animation-frame callbacks during the\n   185\t  stream, summarized per run; the threshold is 1.5× the refresh interval the page observed (25 ms at 60 Hz).\n   186\t- `createTaskMs`: task time to create the initial window and settle.\n   187\t- `neverVisible` (recorded, not compared): samples never shown within 5 s of the last one.\n   188\t\n   189\t**Theme** (primary: `taskMsPerToggle`): per toggle, `TaskDuration` from the class toggle to a fixed window end\n   190\t(three animation frames plus 50 ms), median over 20 toggles. After each window the marks' colors are read: the\n   191\tcomputed `stroke` of every SVG path, and the four most frequent fully opaque colors of every canvas, read from a copy\n   192\tso the chart's canvas is never read back. They must alternate between two different sets; otherwise the run is not\n   193\tadmitted (uPlot, which has no theme mechanism, fails this). `canvasCallsPerToggle` counts Canvas 2D drawing calls\n   194\t(stroke, fill, fillRect, strokeRect, fillText, strokeText, clearRect, drawImage, putImageData) in the window, and\n   195\t`mutationsPerToggle` the DOM mutation records inside the chart: an SVG renderer that repaints through CSS shows zero\n   196\tcanvas calls. `framesToNewColor` is the largest number of frames until the colors changed, from four separate\n   197\tuntimed toggles sampled every frame.\n   198\t\n   199\t**Resize** (primary: `taskMs` over the animation until the charts report ready): plus frame intervals during the\n   200\tanimation (p95, p99, maximum, count over 1.5× the refresh interval), and the renderer and GPU process CPU time. After\n   201\tthe animation every chart must fill its container (widest canvas or svg within 90–100% of its width), or the run is\n   202\tnot admitted. uPlot does not follow its container by itself; its adapter adds a ResizeObserver that calls `setSize`.\n   203\t\n   204\t**Memory** (W-first and W-page; sampled outside the timed window, after `HeapProfiler.collectGarbage`), four\n   205\tfigures that are never added together:\n   206\t- `heapChartBytes`: JS heap used with the chart minus with only the input data on the page (`heapUsedBytes`: absolute);\n   207\t- `rendererPssChartBytes` / `rendererPssBytes`: proportional set size of the page's renderer process (Linux\n   208\t  `/proc/<pid>/smaps_rollup`; CDP has no per-process memory). The page's renderer is the renderer that used the most\n   209\t  CPU time during the run (`SystemInfo.getProcessInfo`); Chrome's other renderers are recorded separately;\n   210\t- `gpuPssChartBytes` / `gpuPssBytes`: the same for the GPU process;\n   211\t- `canvasBackingBytes`: **calculated**, not measured: width × height × 4 over the chart canvases in device pixels.\n   212\t`domNodes` counts the elements in the chart containers. Run with `--dpr 2` for pixel ratio 2.\n   213\t\n   214\t**Process CPU** (`rendererCpuMs`, `gpuCpuMs`): CPU time of the whole renderer and GPU processes over the window, from\n   215\t`SystemInfo.getProcessInfo`. The resolution observed here is 10 ms, so it only means something over long windows.\n   216\t\n   217\t**CPU profiles** (`profile.mjs`): one CDP `Profiler` profile (100 µs sampling) from the create call to the end of the\n   218\tsettle window. Each sample goes to the deepest Axylo frame on its stack and that frame's source module gives the\n   219\tstage. For the development build the module comes from esbuild's module comments; for the production build only\n   220\twhen rebuilding the current sources reproduces the measured bundle byte for byte (then through a source map), and\n   221\totherwise the summary says it is not available. Profiled runs are never timings.\n   222\t\n   223\t**Environment record**, in every history file: Chrome version and whether it was headless, Playwright and Node\n   224\tversions, CPU model and logical core count, cpufreq governor, memory, OS, load average at start and end, the WebGL\n   225\trenderer string, device pixel ratio and the refresh rate the page observed (per run), library versions, lockfile and\n   226\tbundle hashes, the fixture checksum. Things this harness cannot read say \"not available\" with the reason (the commit,\n   227\twhen the checkout is not a git repository; power settings).\n   228\t\n   229\tWhere a metric cannot be measured the result says `not available: <reason>`. No other number is put in its place.\n   230\t\n   231\t## Statistics\n   232\t\n   233\t- Runs come in blocks of two (AB or BA), as many AB as BA, in a seeded random order recorded in the file.\n   234\t- Each run is summarized to one number per metric first; frames and samples are never pooled across runs.\n   235\t- For each metric: the median per configuration, and a paired bootstrap 95% interval (10,000 resamples of whole\n   236\t  blocks) for median(B) − median(A).\n   237\t- Outcome, for each metric: `better` if the whole interval is below zero, `worse` if above, otherwise\n   238\t  `inconclusive`. With `--noise`, the interval must also clear that A-versus-A interval. With fewer than 6 complete\n   239\t  pairs the interval is shown but the outcome is always `inconclusive`.\n   240\t- `--aa` puts one adapter in both slots. Its interval is the noise band of that workload for this session and block\n   241\t  count. The plan asks for bands from at least three sessions; one session is one sample of the band.\n   242\t- \"Inconclusive\" never means \"no difference\".\n   243\t\n   244\t## History files\n   245\t\n   246\t`bench/history/<run id>.json`, written atomically after every run. `status` is `interrupted` from the moment the\n   247\tfile is created and becomes `complete` only after the last block has been analysed, so a run cut short by a restart\n   248\tor a kill stays marked `interrupted` (a signal also records which). `aborted` means the runner itself failed. Each run\n   249\trecord has its `outcome`: `ok`, `not-admitted`, `not-available`, `error`, `timeout` or `crash`, with the reason,\n   250\tconsole errors, raw CDP metric deltas, the adapter's readback, and for streaming the per-sample arrays (scheduled,\n   251\tsent, received, visible, append time) and every frame timestamp. Admission runs are kept with `phase: \"admission\"`.\n   252\t\n   253\t## Limits\n   254\t\n   255\t- Headless Chrome on this machine uses SwiftShader, a software WebGL rasterizer (recorded in every file). Nothing here\n   256\t  says anything about GPU paths; canvas 2D raster also differs from a headed browser with a GPU. `--headed` exists\n   257\t  but has not been used for the recorded runs.\n   258\t- One machine, one browser (Chrome stable). No Firefox, WebKit, Edge, throttled profile or phone yet (plan 3.2).\n   259\t- The machine was not idle: other agents and builds run on it. The load average is recorded.\n   260\t- Task time is main-thread only; no GPU raster time, no traces, no Long Animation Frames. Memory is Linux PSS plus a\n   261\t  calculated canvas figure, not Chrome's own accounting; no leak (create–destroy cycle) measurement yet.\n   262\t- Readiness comes from each library's own hooks; screenshot validation of readiness is not implemented.\n   263\t- Noise bands come from one session.\n   264\t- Not built yet from the plan: rows vs typed columns, irregular x, 10×100k, the mixed page, W-replace, W-hover, W-keys,\n   265\t  W-zoom, W-busy, W-types, W-load, the leak part of W-mem, bytes, the CI smoke job, and the documentation history page.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
