{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba docs/01-landscape.md | sed -n '341,383p'; nl -ba docs/04-plan-performance.md | sed -n '153,205p'; nl -ba docs/02-product-spec.md | sed -n '235,255p'; rg -n 'Canvas|SVG|M0|M1|benchmark|performance smoke' docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md\"","aggregated_output":"   341\t### 5.3 Load\n   342\t\n   343\tTwo metrics from headless Chrome 157 on one desktop machine, median of three runs.\n   344\t\n   345\t- **Main-thread task time to draw**: time the browser reports its main thread busy, from the create call until 1.6 s after the second animation frame, long enough for entry animations to finish. It counts work however the library schedules it. The first row is the same measurement with no chart.\n   346\t- **Main-thread task time per update** while streaming: the same counter over 300 updates, divided by 300.\n   347\t\n   348\t| Library and configuration | 10,000 points | 100,000 points | 1,000,000 points | Streaming: main-thread time per update | Frame callbacks later than 20 ms (of 300) |\n   349\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n   350\t| No chart (measurement floor) | 8 ms | 8 ms | 9 ms | 0.1 ms | 0 |\n   351\t| Chart.js, defaults | 1.2 s | 2.3 s | 13.7 s | 10.7 ms | 2 |\n   352\t| Recharts, defaults | 2.5 s | 9.4 s | no result in 45 s | 15.2 ms | 17 |\n   353\t| D3 (authored: one SVG path, x axis redrawn) | 27 ms | 87 ms | 521 ms | 1.1 ms | 0 |\n   354\t| ECharts, defaults | 1.4 s | 2.8 s | 12.9 s | 15.7 ms | 30 |\n   355\t| Highcharts, defaults | 114 ms | 399 ms | 2.7 s | 3.5 ms | 0 |\n   356\t| ApexCharts, defaults | 83 ms | 134 ms | 636 ms | 9.7 ms | 3 |\n   357\t| Plotly.js, defaults | 51 ms | 119 ms | 365 ms | 6.1 ms | 0 |\n   358\t| Nivo, defaults | 733 ms | 5.0 s | no result in 45 s | 27.1 ms | 247 |\n   359\t| MUI X Charts, defaults | 99 ms | 366 ms | 3.0 s | 4.0 ms | 0 |\n   360\t| uPlot, defaults | 41 ms | 52 ms | 97 ms | 1.0 ms | 0 |\n   361\t| Chart.js, tuned configuration | 75 ms | 73 ms | 165 ms | 2.0 ms | 0 |\n   362\t| ECharts, tuned configuration | 65 ms | 119 ms | 512 ms | 2.2 ms | 0 |\n   363\t| Recharts, tuned configuration | 104 ms | 563 ms | 5.9 s | 4.7 ms | 0 |\n   364\t| Highcharts, Boost module (WebGL, software-rendered here) | 103 ms | 153 ms | 513 ms | 2.8 ms | 0 |\n   365\t| Plotly.js, `scattergl` (WebGL, software-rendered here) | 215 ms | 935 ms | 8.2 s | 17.3 ms | 23 |\n   366\t\n   367\tHow far these numbers can be trusted:\n   368\t\n   369\t- Three runs on one machine. Read them for order of magnitude.\n   370\t- Main-thread time does not include rasterization done on other threads, which matters for large SVG paths.\n   371\t- The streaming producer waits for an animation frame before each update, so a slow library receives a slower feed, and \"later than 20 ms\" counts late callbacks, not dropped frames.\n   372\t- The first version reported only the synchronous part of each update call. By that measure uPlot took under 0.1 ms; its redraw is deferred, and the real cost is about 1 ms.\n   373\t- The first version's D3 rows were invalid: the authored chart pinned its y axis at zero while the test data went negative, so most of the line was outside the plot. The rows above are from the corrected chart. Its y axis still does not follow the stream.\n   374\t- The tuned configurations are whole configurations. Chart.js' changes input format, parsing, decimation, animation, markers and stroke width; Recharts' removes the legend and grid. They show what a different configuration costs, not what any one option does.\n   375\t- The two WebGL rows ran on SwiftShader, a software rasterizer (`spot-checks.json`). They say nothing about WebGL on a GPU.\n   376\t- No adapter except D3's was checked for showing the right domain and window after the fact.\n   377\t\n   378\t**D8. Whole configurations differ by one to two orders of magnitude.** Chart.js: 13.7 s by default against 0.17 s tuned at a million points. ECharts: 12.9 s against 0.5 s. The cause is not isolated to one option.\n   379\t\n   380\t**D9. Plain JavaScript on Canvas 2D sets a high bar.** uPlot drew a million points in about 0.1 s of main-thread time and updated a 600-point window in about 1 ms. Any claim that another technology is needed has to beat numbers of that order.\n   381\t\n   382\t**D10. One DOM node per point is slow; SVG as such is not.** Recharts and Nivo, which create an element per point by default, took 5 to 9 seconds at 100,000 points. The authored D3 chart and Plotly's default trace, which draw one SVG path per series, took about 0.1 s. Nivo's canvas component was not tested.\n   383\t\n   153\t## 7. Where Rust could be justified, and what it would take\n   154\t\n   155\tNo Rust experiment starts before TypeScript 1.0 has shipped and its bottlenecks have been measured.\n   156\t\n   157\t### 7.1 Candidates\n   158\t\n   159\tA Rust experiment is worth running only for a stage that is a measured bottleneck on a tracked workload and is compute-bound on numeric data. Stages that mostly call browser APIs cannot gain: each call crosses the JavaScript–WebAssembly boundary and the browser does the work either way.\n   160\t\n   161\t| Stage | Could Rust help? | Reasoning |\n   162\t|---|---|---|\n   163\t| Simplification and aggregation over typed columns (per-column extremes, binning, cumulative sums) at 1M+ points | Possibly | Tight numeric loops over contiguous memory; SIMD is available. JavaScript engines already compile such loops well. |\n   164\t| Spatial index build and query for scatter at 1M+ points | Possibly | Memory layout control matters; no browser API involved. |\n   165\t| Parsing large text inputs into columns | Unlikely | Strings must be copied into WebAssembly memory and decoded; the copy often costs as much as the parse. |\n   166\t| Layout, ticks, label fitting | No | Small inputs; dominated by text measurement, a browser call. |\n   167\t| Canvas 2D or DOM drawing | No | One boundary crossing per command. |\n   168\t| Rendering to a pixel buffer or to WebGL/WebGPU from Rust | Only as a separate product decision | It replaces the renderer, brings its own text rendering, and does nothing for the DOM-based accessibility layer. |\n   169\t\n   170\t### 7.2 Preconditions for starting\n   171\t\n   172\t1. **The arithmetic allows a win.** If the stage is a fraction *f* of the workload's critical path and Rust makes it *s* times faster, the workload improves by at most *f* × (1 − 1/*s*), before any overhead. A stage at 30% needs a threefold speedup to reach 20%; a stage at 50% needs 1.7 times. The experiment starts only when a measured *f* and a defensible *s* (from a prototype of the kernel alone) give at least 25%, leaving room for transfer and startup.\n   173\t2. **The workload matters.** It fails its budget, or it is the largest remaining wait, at a size in the workload table.\n   174\t3. **TypeScript is already tuned for that stage** (typed arrays, no allocation in the loop, pixel-bounded work), and a reviewer other than the author has confirmed it from a profile.\n   175\t4. **A written hypothesis with a number and a time ceiling.**\n   176\t\n   177\t### 7.3 Evidence required to adopt\n   178\t\n   179\tThe comparison is always end to end, on the workload, against the tuned TypeScript build, with section 3's method.\n   180\t\n   181\t| Question | Evidence required |\n   182\t|---|---|\n   183\t| Is the workload faster for the reader? | The lower bound of the 95% interval for the improvement in the workload's primary metric is at least 20%, and the absolute saving is at least the workload's declared minimum, in Chrome, Firefox and Safari or WebKit. A function-level microbenchmark is not evidence. |\n   184\t| What does startup cost? | Fetch, compile and instantiate time of the module, cold and warm, on desktop and throttled profiles, included in W-load and in the first render of the affected workload. Break-even stated for a page that draws one such chart once (cold) and for one that redraws it repeatedly (warm). |\n   185\t| What does data transfer cost? | Time and peak memory to move columns into WebAssembly memory and results back, included in the end-to-end number. If columns live in WebAssembly memory to avoid copies, the effect on `update`, `append` and P7 is measured too. |\n   186\t| Where is the crossover? | The data size above which the Rust path wins end to end. It must be at or below sizes in the workload table. |\n   187\t| Do small charts pay? | W-first at 3×120, W-page and W-load must not regress beyond tolerance, so the module loads lazily and only above the crossover. The byte, correctness and tail-latency gates of section 5 apply to the module as to any change. |\n   188\t| What happens while it loads, and when it cannot? | `line()` stays synchronous. The TypeScript path draws until the module is ready; the switch happens on a later revision and is invisible. Tested: fetch failure, instantiation blocked by a Content Security Policy without `wasm-unsafe-eval`, `update` and `destroy` during loading, and a stale result arriving after newer data. In every case the chart is correct and no error reaches the page. |\n   189\t| Does it run everywhere Axylo runs? | Script-tag build, Vite, webpack, Next.js. No reliance on threads, which need cross-origin isolation that embedded dashboards rarely have. |\n   190\t| Are both paths the same chart? | Differential tests feed identical inputs to both and compare the selected source indexes, gaps, order and ties exactly, and floating-point results within a stated tolerance; the F2 and F5 property tests run on the Rust path; rendered output is compared within a stated pixel tolerance. |\n   191\t| What does it cost to keep? | Recorded: the toolchain every contributor and CI needs; a native Windows build verified on Windows, not by cross-compiling; lazy bytes; CI minutes; lines of Rust; a named owner for rollback; and the permanent duty to maintain two implementations of one stage. |\n   192\t\n   193\t### 7.4 Decision\n   194\t\n   195\tAdopt only if every row passes. The adoption record names the workload, the crossover size and the conditions for reversal:\n   196\t\n   197\t- tuned TypeScript comes within 10% on a later browser release, checked at each quarterly comparison;\n   198\t- the differential tests cannot be kept green without disproportionate effort;\n   199\t- among charts large enough to be eligible, the fallback path is the one that usually runs.\n   200\t\n   201\tIf the experiment misses, the record is kept with its numbers, the tuned TypeScript stays, and the idea is not reopened without a new profile.\n   202\t\n   203\tA large win on the isolated stage with a small win on the workload is the expected failure. It does not show the profile was wrong: with a stage at 30% of the path, even a perfect kernel saves 30% before copies and startup take their share. The record for such a result measures where the remainder went (transfer, compilation, contention, a bottleneck that moved) before anything else is tried.\n   235\t## 5. Performance budgets\n   236\t\n   237\tThese numbers are provisional. They are design targets taken from Phase 1's order-of-magnitude measurements, and they become release gates only after the benchmark harness built in M0 has measured a working Axylo slice in M1 and the limits have been recalibrated and frozen (plan, M1 and M6). Each budget names its workload, metric and statistic so that two people measuring the same build get the same verdict.\n   238\t\n   239\t| ID | Workload | Metric | Statistic | Provisional limit | Phase 1 reference |\n   240\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n   241\t| P1 | `trend` (3×120), default options, reference desktop | Main-thread task time from the call until the chart is complete | Median of 20 runs | 10 ms | Not comparable across libraries below about 35 ms with the Phase 1 method |\n   242\t| P2 | One series, 100,000 and 1,000,000 points, typed columns | Same | Median of 20 | 60 ms and 250 ms | See 01, section 5.3: uPlot and tuned Chart.js are the fastest measured |\n   243\t| P3 | 600-point window, one appended point per display frame at 60 Hz, driven by an independent clock | Main-thread task time per update; and frames later than 25 ms | Median; count per 300 frames | 2 ms; 0 | See 01, section 5.3 |\n   244\t| P4 | Pointer moved across a 100,000-point chart | Time from input to the frame showing the matching readout | 95th percentile | One frame (16.7 ms at 60 Hz) | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n   245\t| P5 | 24 `trend` charts created on one page | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 200 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n   246\t| P6 | Download | Minified and gzipped bytes, CSS included | Exact | DX7 | 01, section 1.4 |\n   247\t| P8 | 40 compact charts created on one page | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 80 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n   248\t| P7 | Memory | Bytes owned by Axylo beyond the caller's columns for 1,000,000 points; heap trend over 1,000 create–destroy cycles | Measured after forced collection | At most 1× the size of the caller's two columns; no upward slope | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n   249\t\n   250\tThree rules hold whatever the numbers become:\n   251\t\n   252\t1. Budgets apply to default options. Finding D8 showed libraries that are fast only for authors who know which options to set.\n   253\t2. Budgets are absolute, on named hardware profiles. Comparison libraries, uPlot above all, are measured in the same runs and published beside Axylo, but a ratio to another library is not a gate: it would reward leaving out work that library does not do.\n   254\t3. The accessibility layer is on in every measured configuration.\n   255\t\n3:Revision 3, 2026-11-18. Revised after three independent reviews ([ledger](../reviews/2026-11-18/ledger.md)) and the owner's decisions of the same day (scatter and a compact variant are in 1.0; the mark renderer is decided in M1).\n206:**Working assumption: Canvas 2D for marks, HTML for all text.** Text stays in the DOM so that it inherits the page font and color, honors user text spacing and zoom (A6), adapts in forced-colors mode, and can be selected and translated.\n208:**This is reopened and decided in M1 by measurement.** The first draft justified canvas by Phase 1's slow SVG libraries, but those create a node per point; the one-path-per-series SVG chart in the harness was fast. With drawing simplified to a few vertices per pixel column (F5), an SVG path per series is small. SVG follows CSS variables and class-toggled themes with no repaint code, adapts to forced colors, prints sharply, needs no pixel-ratio handling and allocates no backing store (a 1,920×1,080 canvas at pixel ratio 2 is about 33 MB). Canvas is likely cheaper for per-frame redraw when streaming and hovering.\n210:M1 builds both mark renderers behind the same scene and compares them on: P1, P2, P3, P5, bytes, memory, a runtime theme toggle, forced colors, print, and browser zoom. The scene is renderer-neutral either way, so the choice is contained.\n235:Ids are generated per chart. The plot's role, and how a focused point is conveyed, are not fixed here: M1 runs a spike with three candidate patterns and M4 confirms the choice (section 4).\n245:bench/                 benchmark harness (04-plan-performance.md), built in M0\n248:docs/api/              the type-checked API sketch, replaced by generated declarations in M0\n257:Each milestone ends with outcomes checked by a command, an example that runs in the docs site, and the documentation page for what was built. Durations are ceilings for one owner working with coding agents, not targets. Every milestone from M1 on re-runs the acceptance matrix, the size report and the benchmark smoke set, and records the results.\n259:### M0. Foundations (ceiling: 5 days)\n264:- The benchmark harness from the performance plan, section 3: readiness markers, main-thread accounting, the reference-machine record, and adapters for uPlot and Chart.js. It measures those two before any Axylo code exists.\n266:Outcomes: `npm test` and `npm run build` pass on three operating systems; the API report is committed; the benchmark harness reproduces a uPlot and a Chart.js measurement with a recorded noise band.\n268:### M1. A vertical slice, and the two open decisions (ceiling: 12 days)\n272:- **Renderer comparison:** the mark renderer built twice (Canvas 2D and SVG paths) behind the same scene, measured as in 2.3.\n311:- The pattern chosen in M1, built fully: names and summary (A1), keyboard (A2), point text (A3), table with paging (A4), non-color cues (A5), reflow (A6), streaming behavior (A7), messages and language (A10); single-pointer zoom controls (I3, I4); reduced motion on update (I5).\n332:- Scatter: picking, ties, coincident points and a clustered-data benchmark.\n344:- Budgets P1–P5 and P7 measured with the Phase 3 method, recalibrated once if M1's provisional values were wrong, then frozen with a written reason for each.\n390:| The interaction pattern that works in one screen reader fails in another. | Three candidates rehearsed on five pairs in M1, before any of the surrounding DOM is built. Plot roles are internal and can change in a minor release. |\n391:| DOM text costs too much with many charts on a page. | P5 is measured in M1. A fallback to canvas-drawn tick labels would give up user text spacing and forced-colors adaptation for those labels, so it must pass the same L1 and A6 tests before it is allowed. |\n392:| Size targets and features conflict. | Measured from M1; each milestone records its byte cost. Accessibility is not what gets cut; chart types are. |\n395:| One person cannot hand-check real Safari, iOS, Android and Windows screen readers at every milestone. | Automated checks at every milestone; the manual rehearsal at M1, M4 and M8 only, recorded with dates and versions. Device access is a named dependency of those three milestones. |\n407:| Export (PNG, SVG, CSV), print styling | CSV from the table is the first step. |\n408:| Server-side rendering to SVG | The scene makes it possible. |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
