{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_50","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"git show 709fcbf:packages/axylo/src/dom/figure.ts | nl -ba | rg -n 'aria-live|axylo-summary|button|table' | head -20; rg -n 'animate|uPlot|Chart\\\\.js|accessib|no-op' docs/01-landscape.md | tail -30; nl -ba docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md | sed -n '200,222p'; git status --short -- bench docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_50","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"git show 709fcbf:packages/axylo/src/dom/figure.ts | nl -ba | rg -n 'aria-live|axylo-summary|button|table' | head -20; rg -n 'animate|uPlot|Chart\\\\.js|accessib|no-op' docs/01-landscape.md | tail -30; nl -ba docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md | sed -n '200,222p'; git status --short -- bench docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md\"","aggregated_output":"6:     6\t * The keyboard layer, toolbar, legend and table come later; each is a sibling of the plot and gets its own\n84:    84\t  const summary = make('p', `axylo-summary ${HIDDEN}`);\n100:   100\t  live.setAttribute('aria-live', 'polite');\n239:**R8. Dense lines get a marker on every point in four libraries.** Chart.js, Recharts, ECharts and Nivo draw one for each of the 353 values; at 340 px the lines turn into beads.\n241:**R9. Titles and units are thin in the React libraries.** Recharts and Nivo have no chart title. MUI X has a `title` prop that sets an accessible label only. In Recharts and MUI X the y-axis title overlapped the tick labels with the documented placement. Highcharts titles an untitled y axis \"Values\".\n252:All cells are **Observed** in Chrome's accessibility tree and by scripted keyboard and pointer input. No screen reader was run.\n254:| | Chart.js | Recharts | D3 (authored) | ECharts | Highcharts + module | ApexCharts | Plotly.js | Nivo | MUI X | uPlot |\n256:| What the accessibility tree holds | nothing | legend list; an `application` element with no label, containing the tick text | image containing all the chart's text | nothing | named region, heading, generated description, a region per series | named `application`, legend buttons | two unnamed images, a toolbar | unnamed image | legend list, status region; the `title` label sits on a `role=\"none\"` element | title text, legend table |\n263:| Bars animate on first render | yes | yes | no | yes | yes | yes | no | yes | yes | no |\n270:**R11. Half of the ten give a keyboard or assistive-technology user nothing to work with by default.** Chart.js and ECharts put one unlabeled canvas on the page. uPlot adds a title and a legend table that reads `--` until a mouse moves. Nivo is an unnamed image; the authored D3 chart is an image whose only content is its tick text. Chart.js' documentation leaves making the canvas accessible to the author (**Docs**, page dated 2021).\n282:**R14. The bar entry animation ignores reduced motion in four of the seven that animate.** Chart.js, ECharts, Highcharts and Nivo produced the same early-frame difference with `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` as without. None of their packages contains that media query string ([`spot-checks.json`](../research/harness/spot-checks.json)). Recharts, ApexCharts and MUI X stop.\n284:**R15. Tooltips differ in basic behavior.** With the pointer in the plot between points, Chart.js shows nothing; its default requires the pointer to intersect a point. Highcharts, Plotly and Nivo describe one series or point; the others list all series at that x. MUI X's tooltip extended past the container's right edge.\n286:**R16. Three libraries do not follow their container.** ECharts, Plotly and uPlot stayed 720 px wide when the container shrank to 360 px. Each documents a resize call or option. uPlot needs pixel sizes up front.\n296:| Highcharts | Import `modules/accessibility` | One image named by the title; 0 tab stops; a console message recommends the module | Named region, generated description, keyboard navigation, 2 tab stops |\n300:| Chart.js, Plotly.js, Nivo, uPlot, D3 | None found | As in 4.1 | – |\n314:| Chart.js | A date adapter package before a time axis works. `maintainAspectRatio: false` to fill a fixed-height container. |\n318:| Highcharts | The accessibility module is a separate import. |\n323:| uPlot | Pixel width and height, a stroke color per series, x in seconds (or an option to accept milliseconds), a path builder plus a custom axis for bars. |\n329:**D2. Five data shapes for the same table.** Parallel arrays with labels (Chart.js, uPlot), `[x, y]` pairs per series (ECharts, Highcharts, ApexCharts), row objects keyed by series (Recharts), `{ id, data: [{ x, y }] }` (Nivo), and separate x and y arrays (Plotly, MUI X). The harness fixture is columnar and had to be reshaped a different way for each.\n331:**D3. Seven update idioms.** Mutate then `chart.update()` (Chart.js); `setOption` (ECharts); `series.addPoint` (Highcharts); `updateSeries` (ApexCharts); `Plotly.extendTraces`; `setData` (uPlot); re-render with new props (the React three).\n337:**D6. Release cadence ranges from none to very frequent.** Nivo and uPlot have had no release in 18 and 20 months. ApexCharts shipped four major versions in 16 months. Whether either is a problem for a user depends on what broke, which this study did not test.\n339:**D7. Licensing splits the field.** Highcharts is paid for commercial use; ApexCharts is free only below a size threshold; MUI X charges for zoom and pan. The fully permissive libraries include the ones with the weakest accessibility defaults, plus Recharts and MUI X's free tier.\n351:| Chart.js, defaults | 1.2 s | 2.3 s | 13.7 s | 10.7 ms | 2 |\n360:| uPlot, defaults | 41 ms | 52 ms | 97 ms | 1.0 ms | 0 |\n361:| Chart.js, tuned configuration | 75 ms | 73 ms | 165 ms | 2.0 ms | 0 |\n372:- The first version reported only the synchronous part of each update call. By that measure uPlot took under 0.1 ms; its redraw is deferred, and the real cost is about 1 ms.\n374:- The tuned configurations are whole configurations. Chart.js' changes input format, parsing, decimation, animation, markers and stroke width; Recharts' removes the legend and grid. They show what a different configuration costs, not what any one option does.\n378:**D8. Whole configurations differ by one to two orders of magnitude.** Chart.js: 13.7 s by default against 0.17 s tuned at a million points. ECharts: 12.9 s against 0.5 s. The cause is not isolated to one option.\n380:**D9. Plain JavaScript on Canvas 2D sets a high bar.** uPlot drew a million points in about 0.1 s of main-thread time and updated a 600-point window in about 1 ms. Any claim that another technology is needed has to beat numbers of that order.\n389:| Fit every label, or say what is hidden | R2 | Highcharts (wraps); Chart.js (rotates legibly) |\n393:| Fast with default options | D8–D10 | uPlot; Plotly and ApexCharts are within an order of magnitude |\n394:| One data model, one update contract, small bundle, permissive license | D2–D4, D7 | Chart.js on license; uPlot on size |\n396:Among these ten, in the configurations tested, none is strong in more than two rows. The product specification in [02-product-spec.md](02-product-spec.md) is built on holding all of them for a small set of chart types. Two libraries outside the ten, AG Charts and Observable Plot, may be stronger on accessibility defaults and on UTC handling respectively, and should be probed before Axylo claims a gap exists across the whole field.\n   200\t| mark raster | Scene; device pixel ratio; resolved colors (L4's triggers). |\n   201\t\n   202\tText is measured with the page's computed font, letter spacing and word spacing applied to the measuring context, and layout reruns when fonts finish loading. The measurement interface is a parameter, so Node tests inject fixed metrics and browser tests use the real one.\n   203\t\n   204\t### 2.3 Rendering\n   205\t\n   206\t**Working assumption: Canvas 2D for marks, HTML for all text.** Text stays in the DOM so that it inherits the page font and color, honors user text spacing and zoom (A6), adapts in forced-colors mode, and can be selected and translated.\n   207\t\n   208\t**This is reopened and decided in M1 by measurement.** The first draft justified canvas by Phase 1's slow SVG libraries, but those create a node per point; the one-path-per-series SVG chart in the harness was fast. With drawing simplified to a few vertices per pixel column (F5), an SVG path per series is small. SVG follows CSS variables and class-toggled themes with no repaint code, adapts to forced colors, prints sharply, needs no pixel-ratio handling and allocates no backing store (a 1,920×1,080 canvas at pixel ratio 2 is about 33 MB). Canvas is likely cheaper for per-frame redraw when streaming and hovering.\n   209\t\n   210\tM1 builds both mark renderers behind the same scene and compares them on: P1, P2, P3, P5, bytes, memory, a runtime theme toggle, forced colors, print, and browser zoom. The scene is renderer-neutral either way, so the choice is contained.\n   211\t\n   212\t**Simplified drawing.** Per device-pixel column and per contiguous run of valid samples, the stroke covers the run's minimum and maximum (F5). One rule for when it applies: more than four visible samples per device-pixel column on average. The pass over visible samples is O(visible) on typed columns and is measured separately from drawing; the vertex count is bounded by width times the number of runs, not by width alone.\n   213\t\n   214\t**No animation system.** First render is not animated (I5). Updates cut, or tween for at most 200 ms where the cost is negligible, and not at all under reduced motion.\n   215\t\n   216\t### 2.4 Document structure\n   217\t\n   218\t```html\n   219\t<figure class=\"axylo\" id=\"ax7\" role=\"group\" aria-labelledby=\"ax7-t\" aria-describedby=\"ax7-s\">\n   220\t  <figcaption>\n   221\t    <h3 id=\"ax7-t\">Daily sessions by channel</h3>        <!-- level from options; or omitted when labelledBy is used -->\n   222\t    <p id=\"ax7-s\" class=\"axylo-summary\">Line chart, 3 series, …</p>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_51","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"node --input-type=module <<'NODE'\nimport { Decimation } from './bench/node_modules/chart.js/dist/chart.js';\nimport { lineFixture, expectedView, checksum } from './bench/src/fixtures.js';\nimport { admit } from './bench/lib/driver.mjs';\nconst fixture = lineFixture({series:1,points:100000,gaps:true});\nconst original = Array.from(fixture.x, (x, i) => ({x, y: fixture.ys[0][i], i}));\nconst ds = {data:original};\nconst scale = {type:'time', axis:'x', getUserBounds:()=>({minDefined:false,maxDefined:false})};\nconst meta = {xAxisID:'x',controller:{supportsDecimation:true},iScale:scale};\nDecimation.beforeElementsUpdate({width:800,data:{datasets:[ds]},scales:{x:scale},options:{parsing:false,indexAxis:'x'},getDatasetMeta:()=>meta}, {}, {enabled:true,algorithm:'min-max'});\nlet bracketed = [];\nfor (let k=1;k<ds.data.length;k++) {\n const l=ds.data[k-1], r=ds.data[k];\n if (Number.isFinite(l.y)&&Number.isFinite(r.y) && original.slice(l.i+1, r.i).some(p=>Number.isNaN(p.y))) bracketed.push([l.i,r.i,+(800*(r.x-l.x)/(fixture.x.at(-1)-fixture.x[0])).toFixed(3)]);\n}\nconst e=expectedView(fixture);\nconst readback = {x:[e.oldestX,e.newestX],y:[e.yMin,e.yMax],series:[{points:ds._data.length, oldestX:ds._data[0].x,newestX:ds._data.at(-1).x, missing:ds._data.filter(p=>Number.isNaN(p.y)).length,drawnPoints:ds.data.length}]};\nconsole.log('Chart.js pinned decimator, existing fixture + 800px',JSON.stringify({held:ds._data.length,kept:ds.data.length,heldNaNs:readback.series[0].missing,retainedNaNs:ds.data.filter(p=>Number.isNaN(p.y)).length,adjacentKeptFinitePairsStraddlingAGap:bracketed.length,examples:bracketed.slice(0,6),wideExamples:bracketed.filter(x=>x[2]>=1).slice(0,4),admission:admit({workload:'first',params:{size:'1x100000',gaps:true}},{checksum:checksum([fixture]),describe:[readback]},false)}));\nNODE\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
