{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"agent_message","text":"**Decision 0002 should be provisional.** The M1 data supports keeping SVG as the working default for the line-chart slice while retaining Canvas. It does not establish a shipping winner, a P3 pass, or a reason to remove Canvas.\n\nThis was a read-only review. I ran the Node tests, replayed history through the CLI, and ran counterexamples in Node. Browser behavior was assessed from source and recorded runs. The test glob also includes a suite that rebuilds `bench/dist` and launches Chrome, so I ran `node --test --test-isolation=none bench/test/stats.test.mjs`.\n\n```text\nℹ tests 12\nℹ suites 0\nℹ pass 12\nℹ fail 0\nℹ cancelled 0\nℹ skipped 0\nℹ todo 0\nℹ duration_ms 57.184433\n```\n\n## Findings\n\n1. **B · Decision 0002 treats exploratory results as a shipping decision.** **Ran Node on history and inspected the plan.** The 3×120 and 100,000-point create intervals include zero, the DPR 1 dashboard interval falls inside the session noise band, and the million-point comparison has four pairs. The 10,000-point create and DPR 2 dashboard intervals allow improvements as small as **0.16 ms** and **6.28 ms**, respectively. M1 gives verdicts on 149 Canvas–SVG metric comparisons, many correlated, with no adjustment for selecting favorable results. The decision also lacks declared minimum gains and regression tolerances, evidence that tight-budget workloads are safe, and the byte, fidelity and complexity checks required by §5. **Fix.** Keep the default provisional. Declare primary endpoints and thresholds before rerunning. Use simultaneous or multiplicity-adjusted inference for claims across workloads, and require the remaining §5 checks before shipping or removing a renderer. [Decision 0002](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md:9) · [M1 results](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md:77) · [Shipping rules](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/04-plan-performance.md:124)\n\n2. **B · The streaming and resize results cannot establish that no frame was late.** **Ran Node on raw history and synthetic timestamps.** P3’s initial chart and settle have a separate window. The stream counter starts before scheduling the worker feed and ends when a page rAF callback sees the final draw record. It includes dispatch, append, deferred main-thread work and the harness’s tracking. It divides that total by submitted samples, then takes the median across runs. That matches the plan’s aggregate-per-update definition. If P3’s “median” means the median cost of individual updates, it is a different statistic. The worker’s 60 Hz timer is independent, but a draw record read in rAF precedes presentation, and coalesced samples are credited at the same callback. Some M1 runs counted 299 intervals for 300 samples. A synthetic 150 ms interval straddling the resize window boundary yielded **zero** late intervals. **Fix.** Define the intended P3 statistic explicitly, count a full window including boundary-spanning intervals, and validate which sequence actually reached presentation. Scope the current zero to the recorded callback intervals. [Streaming window](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.js:130) · [Frame summaries](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/stats.js:117) · [P3](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:243) · [Decision 0002](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md:25)\n\n3. **B · The production profiles cannot attribute the 100,000-point bottleneck.** **Ran Node on the raw profiles and inspected the mapper.** Production leaves **73.1% Canvas and 71.0% SVG** of busy time unmapped. GC is identifiable at **16.8% and 22.7%**. The **37.4% and 43.2%** attributed to normalization and **1.7% and 2.6%** attributed to drawing come from development builds. The deepest Axylo frame gets the sample even when a native callee is the leaf, so this is neither JS self time nor inclusive time for each ancestor. It cannot recover unreported inlined frames, attribute root GC to an allocator, or measure off-thread raster. The window also includes the settle period, and there is no uPlot profile to explain the gap. **Fix.** Archive the exact production maps, report self and inclusive samples separately, and join stage spans with presentation and raster traces. Keep normalization as a hypothesis from the dev runs. [Profile window](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/driver.mjs:147) · [Profile attribution](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/profile-summary.mjs:94) · [100,000-point profiles](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md:492) · [Decision 0002](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md:39)\n\n4. **B · Admission accepts charts whose drawing has lost fixture features.** **Ran Node against the installed Chart.js decimator and the real admission function.** On the 100,000-point gappy fixture at 800 px, the tuned decimator retained 2,981 points and 60 of 5,000 NaNs. I found **144 adjacent retained finite pairs** spanning a missing value in the original input. Admission passed because it reads the held dataset. The historical tuned run also reports 2,981 drawn points and admits it. A synthetic readback with correct extents, counts and timestamps and no `missing` field passed too, with no drawing required to construct it. The domain checks catch the Phase 1 pinned-axis failure, but extent containment does not prove that the extreme or gaps were drawn. **Fix.** Require missing-value readback and validate the rendered marks or selected source indices for gap breaks, extremes and displayed windows. Sample presented frames against untimed references. [Admission predicate](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/admission.js:24) · [Chart.js held-data readback](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/adapters/chartjs-common.js:24) · [Recorded gappy comparison](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/history/20261118T010745Z-first-1x100000-gaps-uplot-vs-chartjs-tuned-2d3a.json) · [Harness admission claim](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/README.md:3)\n\n5. **B · The resize CPU result cannot pin the cost on raster, and the memory claim drops its denominator.** **Ran Node on history and inspected process sampling.** The 100,000-point resize’s **1,035 → 1,770 ms** is whole-renderer CPU. It does not identify a raster stage, and the SwiftShader string identifies the WebGL probe rather than the Canvas 2D or SVG raster path. The resize CPU trade-off, DPR 2 timing and process-memory differences need a headed GPU-backed rerun before transfer. “Roughly half” applies to the **DPR 2 increase above data-only renderer PSS** at 34.23 → 17.71 MiB. Absolute renderer PSS was 74.65 → 56.21 MiB, and the DPR 1 increase was 23.11 → 18.38 MiB. P7 is still unmeasured. The million-point heap and process deltas and `width × height × 4` estimate cannot identify Axylo-owned bytes. The harness does no replacement-peak measurement or 100/300/1,000-cycle leak and listener check. **Fix.** Trace stage and process ownership on the headed runs, label the PSS quantities precisely, and measure owned buffers and lifecycle retention separately. [Process sampling](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/driver.mjs:188) · [PSS results](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md:341) · [P7 method](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/04-plan-performance.md:54) · [Decision 0002](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/decisions/0002-mark-renderer.md:29)\n\n6. **M · Replaying a recorded comparison silently removes its noise band.** **Ran the CLI.** `node bench/analyze.mjs 20261118T023718Z-page-3x120-x24-axylo-canvas-vs-axylo-svg-41e7` printed the original two noise IDs while calling the **−22.01 ms** task result **better**. Supplying those same IDs explicitly with `--noise` restored **inconclusive**, as published. The CLI initializes `noise` to `null` and recomputes the verdict while retaining the old label. **Fix.** Print the stored analysis by default. On reanalysis, resolve and validate the recorded bands or explicitly request and label a zero-only analysis. Print the band actually used per metric. [Analyzer](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/analyze.mjs:12) · [Formatter](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/report.mjs:13) · [Published dashboard result](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md:154)\n\n7. **M · Failed work can win the speed comparison.** **Ran two Node counterexamples.** A stream with the correct final held window but two sequences never acknowledged by the draw reader passed admission after the watchdog. Eight such candidate runs beat eight complete baseline runs **0.50 vs 2.00 ms/update**, with interval **[−1.50, −1.50]**. Separately, a 20-block run where B timed out 14 times and took 5 ms on the six survivors beat A at 10 ms on every run. Failures are reported separately, but neither the inner stream watchdog nor differential exclusion prevents a primary speed verdict. **Fix.** Give unfinished streams a failure outcome. Make completion and deadline reliability gate speed, and publish survivor-only timing as conditional. Track adverse-run rates too. In a third probe, two candidate runs with 60 late intervals each still gave the same median count and **[0, 0]** interval as a clean baseline. [Stream watchdog](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.js:187) · [Driver admission](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/driver.mjs:80) · [Complete-pair filter](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/stats.js:214)\n\n8. **M · The create workloads are proxies for P1, P2 and P5.** **Inspected source and ran Node on history.** The CDP start snapshot precedes a fresh task and the first create call. The end follows all adapter readiness promises, one more rAF callback and a 500 ms settle. The window includes adapter conversion, chart creation, main-thread script, style, layout and any main-thread paint work charged during it. Fixture generation, checksum, adapter load/setup, empty-container layout and forced memory collections are outside. `readyFrameMs` is sampled in the callback before paint. Work on other threads or after the settle has no coverage. Axylo also scans the held frame through `describe()` inside the window, once on create and on each stream append’s readiness, even though create does not need streaming visibility. P2 uses the right typed-column sizes, with 8 and 4 pairs. P1/P5 use synthetic walks named `s0`–`s2`, whereas the named `trend` has real label lengths and a seven-day gap. **Fix.** Use the named fixture, the specified 20 runs, a constant-time commit hook, and sampled screenshots and traces to validate readiness and settle boundaries. [Create window](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.js:89) · [Preparation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.js:356) · [Axylo instrumentation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/adapters/axylo-common.js:76) · [Named `trend` fixture](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/research/harness/src/data.js:9)\n\n9. **M · Rebuilding from current source can measure SVG against itself.** **Ran the adapters against a Node spy and traced the renderer branch.** Canvas advertises `renderer=canvas` while omitting `__renderer` from the call. Current source selects SVG unless passed `'canvas'`. The browser test asserts the echoed configuration string, so it can pass with both charts using SVG. This is a rerun defect. The M1 create records show two chart canvases per Canvas chart and zero for SVG. **Fix.** Explicitly select both renderers and require actual renderer identity in the committed readback. Test that identity after a production rebuild. [Adapter create](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/adapters/axylo-common.js:61) · [Current renderer selection](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/chart.ts:72) · [Browser test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/test/admission.test.mjs:85)\n\n10. **M · Noise admission permits mismatched controls and a loaded machine.** **Ran the CLI and inspected history.** The loader accepted the **DPR 1** A/A bands on the **DPR 2** dashboard, changing its task verdict from better to inconclusive. It checks workload and serialized parameters only. It can accept finite intervals from fewer than six pairs, mismatched adapters, builds, settle times or hosts. The replay CLI only warns about a Chrome mismatch. M1 unions percentile intervals from one session for only two workload shapes. That is not a distribution of repeatability across three independent sessions. The recorded load average reached 6.8 while other work ran, with no gate on representative refresh, power, contention or drift. **Fix.** Match controls by build, environment and workload, require sufficient complete pairs across independent sessions, and predeclare what makes a session invalid. Leave uncalibrated metrics exploratory. [Noise loader](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/refs.mjs:23) · [Machine record](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/driver.mjs:42) · [M1 noise calibration](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md:54)\n\n11. **M · Six-pair percentile intervals can give a confident answer in the wrong direction.** **Ran Node.** A is always 10 ms. B is 5 ms with probability 0.49 and 11 ms otherwise, so its true median is 11 ms. With five 5s and one 11, the harness returned **better, [−5, −2] ms**. Five or six 5s occur **10.03%** of the time, and both cases give the wrong direction. The implementation takes 10,000 whole-pair resamples and the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles for `median(B) − median(A)`. The bootstrap seed is fixed at 12345. `--seed` controls block order only, and an invalid order seed silently becomes zero in the PRNG. **Fix.** Validate and record both seeds. Use the pilot to set sample sizes and check coverage on tied, skewed and discrete metrics. Calibrate inference to the chosen estimand and the balanced design before permitting verdicts. [Bootstrap](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/stats.js:13) · [Seed handling](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/run.mjs:47) · [PRNG](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/fixtures.js:8)\n\n12. **M · Equal AB/BA counts leave differential carry-over unidentified.** **Ran synthetic blocks and inspected isolation.** Every first run took 10 ms. Either adapter took 4 ms after A and 12 ms after B, with no adapter effect in the same starting state. Four AB and four BA blocks still called B better with **[−6, −2] ms**. The real runner uses fresh pages and contexts, so there is no same-page chart carry-over. It reuses one Chrome process and the host, and admission always runs A before B. There is no symmetric warm-up or analysis by order. The bootstrap also resamples across both orders without preserving their fixed balance. **Fix.** Define cold and warm states, standardize warm-up, inspect order-specific estimates, and isolate browser processes if a counterbalanced pilot detects carry-over. Preserve order strata in inference. [Run order](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/run.mjs:113) · [Context isolation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/driver.mjs:132) · [Bootstrap sampling](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/stats.js:26)\n\n13. **M · Resize and theme can admit incomplete visual responses.** **Reasoned from source and checked the recorded counters.** A chart fixed at the initial 1,200 px can pass resize admission because the box returns to 1,200 px and only the widest graphic is checked afterward. uPlot’s `ready` promise is the already-resolved initial draw promise. A busy adapter can also receive fewer resize steps because width advances by elapsed time. For theme, changing one stroke can satisfy the alternating-signature test while other marks stay stale. The timed counters instrument Canvas calls and DOM mutations, and between-toggle canvas readbacks add asymmetric work. Both M1 renderers recorded six mutations per toggle. Zero Canvas calls for SVG does not establish that SVG required no runtime update or repaint. **Fix.** Validate intermediate and final committed geometry and expected colors per mark. Measure counters and pixel sampling in separate runs, and compare presentation latency alongside task cost. [Resize end check](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.js:313) · [uPlot readiness](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/adapters/uplot.js:35) · [Theme timing](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.js:239) · [M1 theme results](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/results/m1-renderer-comparison.md:190)\n\n14. **M · Required budget workloads and decision-sensitive cases are missing.** **Inspected workload dispatch and enumerated history in Node.** P4 has no real pointer moves on the 100,000-point chart, matching-readout hook, or input-to-presentation p95. P8 has no 40-chart compact workload. `--charts 40` creates ordinary charts. The exact P1/P5 `trend` workloads and the P7 ownership and lifecycle runs are also missing. P6 has a separate size script, but no packed-entry comparison or Canvas-retention byte result in M1. Dense or clustered scatter, 10×100,000, and pan and zoom have no renderer measurements. Resize and theme **were measured** in headless Chrome, and one million points **was measured** with four pairs. The plan’s row/irregular-x variants, mixed dashboard, larger streaming windows, replace, keys/table, busy page, other chart types, cold load, browser matrix, forced colors, print and browser zoom lack recorded M1 evidence. **Fix.** Add the missing budget probes and prioritize the absent or underpowered cases that could reverse the renderer choice. [Workload dispatch](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/refs.mjs:5) · [Performance budgets](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:239) · [Planned workloads](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/04-plan-performance.md:18) · [M1 acceptance scope](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:204) · [Size script](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/scripts/size.mjs:1)\n\n15. **M · The cross-library numbers compare different reader experiences.** **Inspected adapters and checked history in Node.** Inputs, outer CSS boxes, viewport and requested DPR are shared. Plot geometry and fonts differ. uPlot reserves 30 px for its legend and supplies sizes, strokes, millisecond units and a resize observer where needed. It offers a cursor and live legend without Axylo’s tooltip and accessibility layer. Chart.js uses its own canvas font and has tooltips in both configurations, but the adapter provides no equivalent accessibility layer. Its starter configuration includes a time adapter, markers and animations. The declared tuned one changes parsing, input shape, decimation, markers, animation and tooltip hit behavior. Axylo M1 used the minified script-tag bundle with the benchmark hook enabled and no accessibility opt-out. The M1 write-up omits the specific feature differences behind the uPlot gap. The no-op is a harness floor, measured earlier at about 1.26 ms for small create and 0.17 ms/update. **Fix.** Publish the feature matrix alongside starter results, add a matched-reader-task comparison with aligned fonts and animation, and keep the no-op as a contemporaneous control. [Page sizing](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/page.html:7) · [uPlot options](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/adapters/uplot.js:3) · [Chart.js starter](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/adapters/chartjs.js:1) · [Chart.js tuned](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/adapters/chartjs-tuned.js:1) · [Axylo options](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/src/adapters/axylo-common.js:50)\n\n16. **M · History records comparisons but does not detect regressions.** **Inspected the runner, analyzer and CI.** The runner compares adapter references served from one snapshot. The analyzer replays one record. Neither builds the merge base and candidate together, retries a flagged PR, confirms against the last release, or handles a Chrome or host change by rerunning both sides. A sequence of sub-tolerance regressions can accumulate indefinitely without a release comparison. CI invokes tests and a size report, with no paired performance job or `--check` on size. **Fix.** Run two immutable build artifacts in the same balanced job. Add the screening retry, nightly reference-machine confirmation and a last-release comparison. Rebaseline by rerunning both builds on a changed environment. [Runner build selection](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/run.mjs:102) · [CI](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/.github/workflows/ci.yml:27) · [Regression plan](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/04-plan-performance.md:139)\n\n17. **M · The report’s provenance can change without the measurements changing.** **Ran Node on the 39 listed records and the live manifest.** They share the same recorded production bundle hash but report three live HEADs, `709fcbf7`, `e6e0e195` and `b3c1a4fa`. The report displays the first HEAD and loads the build timestamp from the current `dist/manifest.json`, which already describes a different bundle. The build hashes the source file after copying it, and the server serves a shared directory a concurrent benchmark build can replace. The report generator also chooses the first matching profile from the directory without matching its artifact to the session. **Fix.** Seal a session-specific artifact directory and hash the files actually served. Record source provenance at build time and generate reports solely from pinned run records, manifests and profile IDs. [Snapshot build](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/build.mjs:36) · [Server root](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/lib/driver.mjs:15) · [Report metadata](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/results/make-m1-report.mjs:38) · [Profile selection](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/results/make-m1-report.mjs:161)\n\n18. **m · Several test names assert more than the tests do.** **Ran the pure tests and inspected coverage.** “Widens with noise” has no quieter control. The constant-shift case cannot distinguish the requested estimator from a mean or a median of paired differences. In Node, `A=[1,101,102]`, `B=[100,1,103]` gave a difference of medians of **−1**, while the median paired difference is **+1**. The positive admission fixture builds its readback from the same `expectedView()` under test. There is no direct coverage for analyzer replay, `loadNoise` compatibility, formatted verdict consistency, stream/resize/theme summaries, memory-unavailable paths, profile mapping or actual renderer identity. **Fix.** Add those cases using independent expected values and retain the existing negative admission and lifecycle tests. [Statistics tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/test/stats.test.mjs:24) · [Positive fixture test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/test/stats.test.mjs:69) · [Browser test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/bench/test/admission.test.mjs:85)\n\n## Checks that held\n\n- **Scheduling and isolation.** Source inspection confirmed an independent worker clock, equal scheduled AB/BA counts, and a fresh page and context for each run.\n- **Basic statistics.** Node confirmed whole-pair resampling, per-run summaries, and the stated three-way classification and minimum-pair rule on their tested inputs.\n- **History.** Source and records confirmed distinct outcomes, ordered raw stream samples, and atomic checkpoints that leave interrupted sessions identifiable.\n- **Profile accounting.** In the recorded profiles, GC samples were rooted separately, idle samples were excluded from busy shares, and unavailable production mapping was labeled.\n\n## Keep, weaken, withdraw\n\n- **Keep** the M1 raw contrasts and intervals, the inconclusive calls at 3×120, 100,000 and 1,000,000 points, and decision 0002’s retention of the Canvas switch.\n- **Weaken** “SVG by default” to a provisional choice for the tested line slice. Scope the dashboard memory result to incremental renderer PSS at DPR 2, the frame result to observed callbacks, and the uPlot timing gap to the configurations measured.\n- **Withdraw** the claims that raster explains the resize CPU gap, that simplification bounds every chart by width alone, that the SVG theme change needs no runtime work, and that production normalization and GC explain the 100,000-point gap or settle the performance budgets. The recorded heap, DOM and layout results also contradict the claim that only resize CPU favored Canvas."}}
